  "sud_notes": "SUD (Surface-Syntactic UD) reuses the UD morphological FEATS inventory almost unchanged: Aspect, Case, Definite, Degree, Gender, Mood, Number, NumType, Person, Polarity, Poss, Reflex, Tense, VerbForm and Voice are all shared with UD, and the SUD guidelines explicitly defer to the UD feature documentation for their definitions. SUD does not rename or drop any UD feature. FIVE features have their own dedicated SUD guideline page and are therefore marked layer=SUD in this file: ExtPos, Foreign, Shared, Subject and Typo. Three of them overlap with UD (ExtPos, Foreign, Typo exist in the UD universal inventory too) but SUD documents them explicitly, so the SUD page is treated as authoritative here; their permitted value sets do not differ from UD: Foreign=Yes only, Typo=Yes only (SUD additionally expects a companion CorrectForm feature on the same token), and ExtPos is not given an enumerated value list on its SUD page (SUD, like UD, uses UPOS tags as the ExtPos value indicating the functional part of speech of an idiom/title/multiword unit, so the UPOS-tag value set from UD is retained). ExtPos, Foreign and Typo keep their UD leaf category 'Other' (a sensible fit: they are token-level/other features rather than nominal or verbal inflection). Two features are genuinely new to SUD and have no UD counterpart, placed in the added 'SUD-specific' category: (1) Shared (Yes = the dependent attaches to the coordination head and is shared across conjuncts; No = it belongs to a single conjunct); and (2) Subject, used on control/raising constructions to record how the subject of a controlled infinitive is instantiated, enumerated at https://guidelines.surfacesyntacticud.org/docs/general_guideline/Features/Subject/ with values SubjRaising, ObjRaising, OblRaising, Generic, Instantiated and NoRaising (NoRaising is deprecated and subsumes Generic/Instantiated); Subject replaces the deprecated @x deep-edge feature of earlier SUD versions. Category grouping follows UD's own index at https://universaldependencies.org/u/feat/: the top-level headings are 'Lexical features' and 'Inflectional features', where the inflectional block is sub-split into 'Nominal', 'Verbal' and 'Other'. Those sub-headings are used verbatim as the category values here (a feature's category is its leaf heading); UD notes these labels are approximate categories only."
